
A quick way to install Xtls ver. 9.0 and its companies to your computer. 
The programs are assumed to install on Unix or Unix-like operating 
system (Linux, Mac OS X etc.) and a Fortran compiler and the C-shell 
(or TC-shell) are also requiered.

1)  The source program of Xtls is written in Fortran. If you do not 
have any of Fortran compiler on your computer, you need to install it. 
Installation of a Fortran 90 or later version of compiler is 
particularly recommended. For FORTRAN77 compiler, procedures are little
different. Read instructions in the file "Makefile", for details.
If you are using GNU g77 compiler see also the last paragraph of 5).

2)  Open the file "Makefile" in this directory with an editor and 
modify the values of variables MODE, BINDIR, FC and FRM according to 
the instructions in the file. At least you need to specify the command
 and options of your Fortran compiler by the variables FC and FRM.

  You can also specify a directory where you want to put the executable
files by BINDIR.  The executable files will be created in a directory 
"bin" under your home directory by default. 

3)  At the bottom of the file x900.sed, you will find several lines 
something like
  
    />>>START GNU/,/>>>END GNU/d

  Read instraction in the file and put "#" begining of the line 
corresponding to your fortran compiler. If you can not identfy which 
line is suited for your system, put "#" at the begining of the line 
with "UNKNOWN".

4) Type the following command and hit the return key.

    make all

  If the programs are successfully compiled, executable files xtls900, 
pre-X15, x900, xcs, xc will be created under the directory specified 
by BINDIR.

5) Set command path to the directory specified by BINDIR. (If you are 
using C-shell or TC-shell, add this directory to the variable "path" 
in .cshrc or .tcshrc files in your home directory and type 
"source ~/.cshrc" or "source ~/.tcshrc" will do.)
   For a test run, type the following command and hit the return key.

    make test

   If all the programs have been properly installed, a messege will 
appear: "Xtls version 9.0 has been successfully installed!".

 If you are using an old compiler (such as GNU g77), sometimes the 
X-code interpreter is not properly installed. To check out this, 
type "xc" and hit the return key. You will find a prompt "Xcode>" 
appears. Input a simple expression such as "1+1" and hit the return 
key. The results must be something like below.
-----
   Xcode interpreter V2.30 coded by Arata Tanaka (Jan. 2004)
Xcode>1+1
           2
Xcode>
-----
To quit the interpreter hold control key and push D key.
If you find error or warning messages every time you hit return key
or the prompt "Xcode>" appears wrong timing, the installation is
not successful.  In such situation, open the files xc230.sed, 
xcs230.sed and xclt230.sed and find a line with
       s/'(A,$)'/'(A)',ADVANCE='no'/g
and comment out the line by putting "#" beginning of it in each of
these files. Erase executable files xc and xcs in the directory
specified by the variable BINDIR and recompile.

